UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 34, Lesson 1: Languages around Australia', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"More than 300 languages are spoken in homes across Australia, according to the 2021 census, and English is the most common. There are hundreds of First Nations languages, many still spoken and being revived by communities. Auslan is the sign language of the Deaf community in Australia.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy might a community want to bring back a language that few people speak?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLanguage carries culture, stories and identity. Reviving a language helps keep these strong for future generations.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving there is only one Aboriginal language, or that First Nations languages no longer exist, ignores the many languages that are still spoken and being revived.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCollect five greetings from languages used in your community with permission, plus one from a published source. Write each with a pronunciation note. For First Nations languages use only local language centre or published sources.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"More than 300 languages are spoken in homes across Australia, according to the 2021 census, and English is the most common. There are hundreds of First Nations languages, many still spoken and being revived by communities. Auslan is the sign language of the Deaf community in Australia.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Collect five greetings from languages used in your community with permission, plus one from a published source. Write each with a pronunciation note. For First Nations languages use only local language centre or publishe Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example: a class collects greetings for hello from five languages used by students and families who agree to share, and writes them with a pronunciation note. For First Nations languages, they use materials from the local language centre and do not guess words.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing there is only one Aboriginal language, or that First Nations languages no longer exist, ignores the many languages that are still spoken and being revived. Correction: Language carries culture, stories and identity. Reviving a language helps keep these strong for future generations.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing there is only one Aboriginal language, or that First Nations languages no longer exist, ignores the many languages that are still spoken and being revived. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"More than 300 languages are spoken in homes across Australia, according to the 2021 census, and English is the most common. There are hundreds of First Nations languages, many still spoken and being revived by communities. Auslan is the sign language of the Deaf community in Australia. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["More than 300 languages are spoken in homes across Australia, according to the 2021 census, and English is the most common. There are hundreds of First Nations languages, many still spoken and being revived by communities. Auslan is the sign language of the Deaf community in Australia.","Believing there is only one Aboriginal language, or that First Nations languages no longer exist, ignores the many languages that are still spoken and being revived.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Believing there is only one Aboriginal language, or that First Nations languages no longer exist, ignores the many languages that are still spoken and being revived.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new inquiry\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Language carries culture, stories and identity. Reviving a language helps keep these strong for future generations. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["Language carries culture, stories and identity. Reviving a language helps keep these strong for future generations.","Believing there is only one Aboriginal language, or that First Nations languages no longer exist, ignores the many languages that are still spoken and being revived.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Why might a community want to bring back a language that few people speak?\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"